# SRG-APP-000435-WSR-000147  -  CAT II
# The web server must be protected from being stopped by a non-privileged
# user.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000435-WSR-000147
group:           V-206432
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       manual
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-002385
control_rev5:    SC-5

contribution:    Splunk Web can only keep its process ID file at the vendor-mandated
                 location inside the Splunk installation so the file inherits the
                 installation's ownership; it cannot set permissions on that file or on the
                 utilities that start and stop the server.

governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          web.conf [settings] pid_path https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Operating system ownership and permission bits on the process ID file and
                 on the splunk start and stop utilities
external:        Service manager (systemd unit or container runtime) that decides which
                 accounts may stop splunkd and splunkweb
external:        Privileged account management procedure that assigns those rights to
                 privileged accounts only
external:        Ownership and mode of the run directory and of the process identifier
                 file, and the service manager restriction on who may stop the splunkd
                 unit, all carried by the operating system.

manual_reason:   The requirement asks that the application be protected from being stopped
                 by an unauthorized user. web.conf [settings] pid_path only names the file
                 splunkweb writes its process identifier to; who may read or write that
                 file, and who may stop the splunkd service, are properties of the
                 filesystem and the service manager. No web.conf setting carries the
                 objective, so the assessor reads the file ownership and the unit
                 permissions instead.

citation:        SRG-APP-000435-WSR-000147 check text; web.conf.spec:1035-1038
finding:         web.conf [settings] pid_path does not hold the vendor-mandated value
                 var/run/splunk/splunkweb.pid, so the Splunk Web process ID file is written
                 outside the Splunk installation directory where the installation's
                 ownership does not protect it.
